1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
Ten yellow pens on a table.

2
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:04,700
Some of them will give
you an electric shock.

3
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:06,980
Nobody will tell you which.

4
00:00:07,695 --> 00:00:11,015
You're a student,
waiting for an experiment to start.

5
00:00:11,755 --> 00:00:14,035
You're told the pens are left
over from an earlier one,

6
00:00:14,265 --> 00:00:16,635
and you can click them to pass the time.

7
00:00:17,201 --> 00:00:20,381
There's no prize.
The only thing a click can get you

8
00:00:20,611 --> 00:00:23,841
is the answer to one question:
does this one shock?

9
00:00:24,488 --> 00:00:27,068
On average, students clicked about five.

10
00:00:27,827 --> 00:00:29,947
A second group got labelled pens.

11
00:00:30,667 --> 00:00:34,467
Red for a shock, green for nothing.
They clicked about three.

12
00:00:35,095 --> 00:00:37,195
The only difference was not knowing.

13
00:00:37,755 --> 00:00:41,075
The researchers tried it again
with chalkboard screeches.

14
00:00:41,155 --> 00:00:44,315
People kept clicking,
and they felt worse for it.

15
00:00:45,416 --> 00:00:48,356
Now take away the pens,
and put a phone in your hand.

16
00:00:48,976 --> 00:00:50,816
You unlock it to check the news.

17
00:00:51,573 --> 00:00:55,053
There's a headline about a war.
Then another article.

18
00:00:55,413 --> 00:00:57,673
Then a video of something terrible.

19
00:00:58,286 --> 00:01:01,006
Forty-five minutes later,
you're still there.

20
00:01:01,686 --> 00:01:05,766
You feel worse than when you started, and
you can't do anything about most of it.

21
00:01:06,277 --> 00:01:10,097
One more article.
One more update. One more video.

22
00:01:10,748 --> 00:01:12,728
This has a name: doomscrolling.

23
00:01:13,108 --> 00:01:17,308
Carrying on through bad news, even though
you know it's making you feel worse.

24
00:01:17,971 --> 00:01:21,651
So why would anyone spend their free time
on something that makes them miserable?

25
00:01:22,351 --> 00:01:26,731
Why doesn't the brain simply say:
enough, let's watch something funny?

26
00:01:27,307 --> 00:01:29,307
There are three parts to the answer.

27
00:01:29,767 --> 00:01:31,367
Two of them are in your head.

28
00:01:31,807 --> 00:01:33,307
The third one isn't.

29
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:38,547
You're walking through a forest,
and you hear something behind a bush.

30
00:01:39,047 --> 00:01:43,727
It might be the wind. It might be a bird.
Or it might be a predator.

31
00:01:44,526 --> 00:01:46,566
Ignore the wind, and nothing happens.

32
00:01:46,646 --> 00:01:49,646
Ignore the predator,
and that's the end of you.

33
00:01:49,966 --> 00:01:52,566
A false alarm costs a few seconds.

34
00:01:53,086 --> 00:01:54,906
A missed threat costs everything.

35
00:01:55,336 --> 00:01:57,076
So the usual story goes,

36
00:01:57,156 --> 00:02:00,796
a brain that overreacts to danger
is the one that survives.

37
00:02:01,676 --> 00:02:04,716
Psychologists call the result
the negativity bias.

38
00:02:05,882 --> 00:02:08,502
And you can see how
it affects what people click.

39
00:02:09,222 --> 00:02:12,122
A news site called Upworthy
used to test its headlines:

40
00:02:12,462 --> 00:02:15,322
same story, different wording,
shown at random.

41
00:02:15,836 --> 00:02:20,136
Researchers later went through
those tests, over twenty thousand of them.

42
00:02:20,976 --> 00:02:25,236
Each extra negative word raised
the click rate by about two percent.

43
00:02:25,316 --> 00:02:28,356
Each positive word
lowered it by about one.

44
00:02:28,949 --> 00:02:31,469
But two percent is a nudge, not a trap.

45
00:02:32,189 --> 00:02:35,409
And in that data,
words of fear actually lowered clicks.

46
00:02:36,169 --> 00:02:39,329
So a brain hunting for predators
can't be the whole story.

47
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:42,013
It might get you to click one headline.

48
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:47,173
You could read it, put the phone away,
and move on. So why don't you?

49
00:02:49,273 --> 00:02:51,313
In 2016, researchers in London

50
00:02:51,543 --> 00:02:54,733
sat forty-five people down
with a game of two rocks.

51
00:02:55,273 --> 00:02:59,193
Each time, you guess:
is there a snake under this one?

52
00:02:59,397 --> 00:03:02,997
If a snake appears,
you get a real electric shock in the hand.

53
00:03:03,237 --> 00:03:05,277
And the odds keep changing.

54
00:03:05,977 --> 00:03:09,197
Sometimes one rock is nearly
always the dangerous one.

55
00:03:09,277 --> 00:03:11,077
Sometimes it's fifty-fifty.

56
00:03:12,277 --> 00:03:15,257
Every few rounds they asked
how stressed people felt.

57
00:03:15,857 --> 00:03:17,557
The shocks mattered, of course.

58
00:03:17,997 --> 00:03:21,077
But stress also rose
and fell with something else.

59
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:22,420
Uncertainty.

60
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:25,340
And uncertainty is highest at fifty-fifty.

61
00:03:26,534 --> 00:03:29,954
As the lead author summed it up,
not knowing whether you'll get a shock

62
00:03:30,184 --> 00:03:33,754
can be more stressful
than knowing you definitely will or won't.

63
00:03:34,182 --> 00:03:37,302
Now imagine a headline about
a war that's just begun.

64
00:03:38,222 --> 00:03:42,222
Something serious is happening,
and you don't know how bad it will get.

65
00:03:42,856 --> 00:03:45,616
Will it escalate?
Will another country get involved?

66
00:03:46,376 --> 00:03:48,716
Will it calm down by morning?
Nobody can tell you.

67
00:03:48,946 --> 00:03:51,516
You don't know the odds. That's the point.

68
00:03:51,936 --> 00:03:53,996
So you look for another article.

69
00:03:54,596 --> 00:03:57,776
It answers some of your questions,
and it raises new ones.

70
00:03:58,213 --> 00:04:01,333
So this may not be about
enjoying the information at all.

71
00:04:01,993 --> 00:04:04,433
It may be about trying
to get rid of not knowing.

72
00:04:05,353 --> 00:04:06,613
Surveys do find that people

73
00:04:06,843 --> 00:04:08,313
who struggle most with uncertainty

74
00:04:08,543 --> 00:04:10,473
tend to doomscroll more.

75
00:04:11,091 --> 00:04:12,491
And here's the trap.

76
00:04:13,191 --> 00:04:15,391
The more you try to eliminate uncertainty,

77
00:04:15,791 --> 00:04:18,971
the more information you find
that creates new uncertainty.

78
00:04:19,701 --> 00:04:23,601
You're trying to reach the point where
you finally understand what's happening.

79
00:04:24,341 --> 00:04:28,041
But there's always another update,
and that point keeps moving.

80
00:04:28,610 --> 00:04:30,730
Every headline is a yellow pen.

81
00:04:33,570 --> 00:04:36,930
That's the two parts in your head.
Here's the third.

82
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:39,760
A newspaper has a last page.

83
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:42,240
A news broadcast ends.

84
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:45,800
Without saying a word,
both of them tell you that you're done.

85
00:04:46,397 --> 00:04:51,557
In 2006, a designer called Aza Raskin
came up with infinite scroll.

86
00:04:52,557 --> 00:04:57,097
It was meant to make browsing easier:
no more clicking through to the next page.

87
00:04:57,802 --> 00:05:00,022
It also got rid of the last page.

88
00:05:00,782 --> 00:05:02,762
He later said he regretted it.

89
00:05:03,182 --> 00:05:06,102
If you don't give your brain time
to catch up with your impulses,

90
00:05:06,182 --> 00:05:07,922
he said, you just keep scrolling.

91
00:05:08,675 --> 00:05:11,115
And what comes next usually isn't random.

92
00:05:11,835 --> 00:05:16,355
Ranking systems try to predict what
you'll click, watch, share or reply to.

93
00:05:16,859 --> 00:05:19,579
In February 2023, researchers had

94
00:05:19,809 --> 00:05:23,539
eight hundred and six Twitter
users install a browser extension.

95
00:05:24,419 --> 00:05:27,219
It captured what the algorithm
was showing them,

96
00:05:27,299 --> 00:05:29,839
and what they'd have
seen in plain time order.

97
00:05:30,375 --> 00:05:32,995
The algorithm's feed had more anger,
above all,

98
00:05:33,075 --> 00:05:35,735
and more hostility
to the other political side.

99
00:05:36,475 --> 00:05:38,755
Asked how each tweet made them feel,

100
00:05:38,835 --> 00:05:42,295
readers reported more anger,
anxiety and sadness.

101
00:05:43,115 --> 00:05:45,595
And, to be fair,
a little more happiness too.

102
00:05:46,198 --> 00:05:47,718
Then came the question.

103
00:05:48,298 --> 00:05:51,138
Do you actually want to be shown
tweets like this one?

104
00:05:51,736 --> 00:05:55,216
Overall, the algorithm's picks
did only slightly better.

105
00:05:55,376 --> 00:05:57,936
And for political tweets, it lost.

106
00:05:58,776 --> 00:06:00,696
People were less keen
on what it chose for them

107
00:06:00,926 --> 00:06:03,396
than on what they'd have
got in plain time order.

108
00:06:03,675 --> 00:06:07,155
So what you react to isn't
necessarily what you want to see.

109
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:10,600
The algorithm doesn't need
to know whether you're happy.

110
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:14,500
It needs to predict whether you'll
interact with the next post.

111
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:17,640
And sometimes, being upset is enough.

112
00:06:18,377 --> 00:06:20,497
That's one platform, in one month.

113
00:06:21,057 --> 00:06:24,097
And yes, this video probably
reached you the same way.

114
00:06:26,587 --> 00:06:28,687
Put the three together,
and you get a loop.

115
00:06:28,987 --> 00:06:31,207
Something disturbing makes you anxious.

116
00:06:32,047 --> 00:06:34,187
Anxiety sends you looking for more.

117
00:06:34,727 --> 00:06:36,847
The next thing you find is a new threat.

118
00:06:37,527 --> 00:06:39,487
But does it actually do you any harm?

119
00:06:40,167 --> 00:06:44,507
Surveys, from Iran to the United States,
link doomscrolling to anxiety.

120
00:06:45,307 --> 00:06:48,607
But perhaps anxious people
simply seek out bad news.

121
00:06:48,996 --> 00:06:52,196
So in 2021, one team tried an experiment.

122
00:06:53,176 --> 00:06:55,736
About nine hundred people
were randomly assigned:

123
00:06:56,036 --> 00:06:59,716
real COVID news on Twitter or YouTube,
or nothing at all.

124
00:07:00,251 --> 00:07:02,631
They got between two
and four minutes of it.

125
00:07:03,231 --> 00:07:05,171
That was enough to lower their mood.

126
00:07:05,791 --> 00:07:09,331
Stories of kindness from the same
pandemic didn't do that.

127
00:07:09,930 --> 00:07:12,930
For the long run,
the best evidence comes from Boston.

128
00:07:13,750 --> 00:07:17,010
Two to four weeks after
the 2013 marathon bombing,

129
00:07:17,090 --> 00:07:20,650
researchers surveyed over four
and a half thousand Americans.

130
00:07:20,950 --> 00:07:23,450
How much coverage had they followed?

131
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:26,390
Some of them were connected
to it directly.

132
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:29,730
They'd been there,
or they knew someone who had.

133
00:07:30,373 --> 00:07:33,333
Others had followed it for six
hours a day or more.

134
00:07:33,925 --> 00:07:36,865
Heavy media exposure
was more strongly associated

135
00:07:37,095 --> 00:07:40,105
with acute stress than direct exposure.

136
00:07:40,869 --> 00:07:44,009
A survey can't prove
that the coverage caused the stress.

137
00:07:44,209 --> 00:07:47,089
So the team kept
following the same people.

138
00:07:47,709 --> 00:07:51,189
Heavy viewers had more stress
symptoms six months later.

139
00:07:51,809 --> 00:07:54,609
Those symptoms went with more
worry about the future.

140
00:07:55,231 --> 00:07:58,811
Then, in 2016,
came the Orlando nightclub shooting.

141
00:07:59,511 --> 00:08:03,711
The people who worried most followed
it more closely, and it hit them harder.

142
00:08:04,374 --> 00:08:05,814
That's still not proof.

143
00:08:06,474 --> 00:08:08,734
But it's the shape a loop
would leave behind.

144
00:08:09,414 --> 00:08:12,214
Anxiety sends you to the news,
the news feeds the anxiety,

145
00:08:12,444 --> 00:08:14,594
and both can be happening at once.

146
00:08:17,524 --> 00:08:20,384
Perhaps the strangest part
is that it feels productive.

147
00:08:20,764 --> 00:08:22,764
You're not wasting time.

148
00:08:22,844 --> 00:08:24,624
You're staying informed.

149
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:26,720
And that's not an illusion.

150
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:32,460
In 2018, an experiment paid some
Americans to quit Facebook for four weeks.

151
00:08:33,145 --> 00:08:35,165
They knew less about the news afterwards.

152
00:08:36,005 --> 00:08:38,915
They reported slightly
higher subjective well-being,

153
00:08:39,085 --> 00:08:41,005
and they had an extra hour a day.

154
00:08:41,284 --> 00:08:43,684
So there's a real cost to looking away.

155
00:08:44,304 --> 00:08:46,804
The question is what
the tenth look gets you.

156
00:08:47,755 --> 00:08:51,015
Say you check the same story
ten times in one evening.

157
00:08:51,655 --> 00:08:53,475
Are you ten times better informed?

158
00:08:54,295 --> 00:08:56,475
You've picked up a few details,
and spent an hour

159
00:08:56,705 --> 00:08:58,675
going over things you can't influence.

160
00:09:01,355 --> 00:09:03,175
So what can you do about it?

161
00:09:03,795 --> 00:09:06,155
The obvious advice is to stop scrolling.

162
00:09:06,235 --> 00:09:09,275
Which is about as useful
as telling someone hungry

163
00:09:09,505 --> 00:09:11,375
to stop thinking about food.

164
00:09:11,994 --> 00:09:15,514
The evidence for fixes is thinner
than the evidence for the problem.

165
00:09:16,294 --> 00:09:19,074
The closest test is about email, not news:

166
00:09:19,494 --> 00:09:22,994
in a week of checking it three times
a day, people felt less stressed.

167
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:25,960
So you could try putting
the last page back.

168
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:29,480
Decide what you want to know
before you open the feed.

169
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:31,480
Pick a time to look.

170
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:35,260
And when the fifth post says
what the fourth one said, stop.

171
00:09:35,830 --> 00:09:38,590
There's one more thing,
and it comes from the pens.

172
00:09:39,550 --> 00:09:41,550
The same researchers
hid pictures of insects,

173
00:09:41,780 --> 00:09:44,030
and let people click to reveal them.

174
00:09:44,568 --> 00:09:46,928
Some were first asked how
they'd feel afterwards.

175
00:09:47,648 --> 00:09:50,108
They clicked less,
and they ended up happier.

176
00:09:50,848 --> 00:09:52,588
Nobody told them to stop.

177
00:09:55,148 --> 00:09:57,088
So why can't you stop scrolling?

178
00:09:57,648 --> 00:10:00,748
Because your brain is doing
something that once made sense.

179
00:10:01,348 --> 00:10:05,088
It's watching for threats,
and trying to find out what happens next.

180
00:10:05,808 --> 00:10:08,388
Those instincts used
to run out of newspaper.

181
00:10:09,108 --> 00:10:10,548
Now they never do.

182
00:10:11,775 --> 00:10:13,775
Being aware of everything
happening in the world

183
00:10:14,005 --> 00:10:16,735
doesn't mean you have
more control over it.

184
00:10:17,275 --> 00:10:21,235
Sometimes it just means you're carrying
more of it around in your head.

185
00:10:21,793 --> 00:10:24,853
You can care about what's happening
without watching it happen.

186
00:10:24,933 --> 00:10:29,273
And the world isn't only what's
terrible on the internet right now.

187
00:10:29,573 --> 00:10:32,373
It's also whatever
is right in front of you.

188
00:10:35,303 --> 00:10:37,203
So here's a question for you.

189
00:10:37,783 --> 00:10:41,263
What do you keep checking,
even though it never changes what you do?

190
00:10:41,583 --> 00:10:43,053
Let me know in the comments.
